 "short_description": "The working heart muscle cell: a branched, roughly cylindrical striated cell about 100-150 um long and 20-35 um wide with usually one central nucleus, regular sarcomeres, T-tubules aligned on the Z-disc, and - its defining feature - intercalated discs at the cell ends whose desmosomes, fascia adherens and connexin-43 gap junctions mechanically and electrically couple it to its neighbours, so the whole sheet beats as one. Mitochondria fill roughly a third of its volume, which is why it is practically tireless.",
